I. Objective
You are an expert in PFAS adsorption studies. Your will be provided with:
(1) Requested Performance_id values that define the target records to extract.
(2) Source chunk(s) from the same paper. These chunks may include experimental protocols, operating conditions, water properties, and performance records needed to extract the requested records.
(3) Figure captions for figures mentioned in the provided chunk(s). Any relevant information they contain is extractable. If no figure is mentioned, or if the relevant figure was not captured in an earlier step, this field will be empty.
(4) Adsorbent_list is provided to help interpret and standardize adsorbent identities, especially abbreviations or coded names. Requested Performance_ids are limited to in-scope adsorbents; do not extract records for out-of-scope adsorbents. However, experimental protocol details described with out-of-scope adsorbents may still be extractable if the same protocol also applies to the in-scope adsorbents, such as when in-scope adsorbents are tested as comparisons under the same conditions.
(5) Water_type_list, a controlled vocabulary used only to standardize water matrix labels when the provided chunks support the match. each entry include the full name, abbreviation, and water type for a single water matrix, if a field is not shown, it means it was not extracted. The provided Water_type_list is a controlled vocabulary for standardizing water labels; it is not evidence by itself. 

Your task is to extract adsorption performance records requested by their Performance_id from the source chunk(s). Each record captures target performance metrics (see II. Extraction Scope) and the associated experimental conditions for a specific combination of PFAS, adsorbent, test mode, and differentiating condition(s). Performance_id is the unique identifier for each performance record and is constructed as: <PFAS_name>|<Adsorbent_id>|<Test_mode>|<Differentiating_Condition>. 

II. Extraction Scope
A candidate performance record is in scope only if it satisfies both conditions below:
1. It reports **absolute numeric values** of **target performance matrices** for PFAS batch adsorption test, during which PFAS-containing liquid is contacted with a freshly prepared adsorbent in a closed-vessel to measure PFAS uptake/removal by adsorption under defined conditions **without** energy input. Regeneration, desorption, reuse-cycle batch tests are out of scope because those tests evaluate the performance of a spent, regenerated, or reused adsorbent rather than the initial adsorption performance of a freshly prepared adsorbent. Similarily, adsorption with energy input such as electricity, ultrasonic irradiation is also out-of-scope and excluded from output. **Target performance metrices** include Freundlich isotherm parameters Kf and 1/n (or n) (Qe=KF.Ce^(1/n)), Langmuir isotherm parameters Qm and KL (Qe=Qm.KL.Ce/(1+KL.Ce)), linear isotherm parameters Kd (Kd=Qe/Ce), pseudo-first-order (PFO) k1 and Qe (dQ/dt = k1 (Qe - Q)), and pseudo-second-order (PSO) k2 and Qe (dQ/dt = k2 (Qe-Q)^2)), standalone solid-phase uptake Qe (Qe=(C0-Ce).V/m), removal rate R (R=(C0-Ce)/C0*100%), and single point Kd. Exclude alternative kinetic or isotherm models such as Elovich, Boyd, Biot, Temkin, Sips (aka Langmuir-Freundlich), dual-mode sorption, and BET. Do not identify these metrics solely by their symbols, because authors may use different notation across studies. Instead, rely on each parameter’s physical meaning, source model, and contextual role. For example, Kd may be written as k1, K, or in another format; if it represents qe/Ce or the fitted coefficient in qe = Kd·Ce, map it to Kd_value regardless of notation. Conversely, symbols such as Qm, qm, KL, K, k2, n, and R2 are out of scope when they come from Langmuir-Freundlich/Sips models, even if the same symbols would be in scope under a standard Langmuir or Freundlich model.

2. Base decisions only on evidence provided in the source chunks and relevant figure captions. The extractable fields must be original to the current study. Do not extract any values from previous work in the literature.  

